      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>The Dr. Aykroyd Formula: How Minimum Pay Is Derived</h2>
        <p>
          Since the 7th CPC, the justification for the fitment factor and the entry-level basic pay floor has relied on the <strong>Dr. Wallace Aykroyd Nutritional Formula</strong>. Recommended by the 15th Indian Labour Conference, this method computes the minimum living wage required for a standard working-class family consisting of three consumption units (husband, wife, and two children):
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>The Four Pillars of the Living Wage Equation:</h4>
          <ul class="editorial-list">
            <li><strong>Food Norm:</strong> A daily consumption of 2,700 calories per adult consumption unit, comprising pulses, cereals, milk, edible oils, meat/fish, and vegetables based on local dietary standards.</li>
            <li><strong>Clothing Norm:</strong> A minimum of 72 yards of fabric per year per family of four.</li>
            <li><strong>Housing Norm:</strong> Rent calculated at 10% of minimum food and clothing expenditures for standard municipal tenement spaces.</li>
            <li><strong>Social Overheads (Supreme Court Judgment):</strong> In the <em>Reptakos Brett & Co.</em> case, the Supreme Court ruled that an additional 25% must be added to cover children's education, medical requirements, domestic fuel, lighting, and recreation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p>
          Applying current retail consumer price indices to the Dr. Aykroyd formula yields an absolute monthly subsistence requirement of approximately <strong>₹38,000 to ₹42,000</strong> for an entry-level public sector worker, providing empirical backing for a fitment multiplier between <strong>2.15 and 2.30</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

