      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Impact on Pensioners: Why the Multiplier Applies Directly to PPOs</h2>
        <p>
          Retirees and family pensioners benefit directly from the approved fitment multiplier. Under Department of Pension and Pensioners' Welfare (DoPPW) conventions, the fitment factor is applied directly to a retiree's existing monthly basic pension:
        </p>
        <ul class="editorial-list">
          <li><strong>Direct Multiplication:</strong> A pensioner receiving a 7th CPC basic pension of ₹20,000 under a 2.28 multiplier receives an 8th CPC basic pension of exactly <strong>₹45,600</strong> per month.</li>
          <li><strong>Minimum Pension Floor:</strong> The statutory minimum pension of ₹9,000 per month climbs to <strong>₹20,520</strong> under the 2.28 factor and to <strong>₹25,740</strong> under the 2.86 factor.</li>
          <li><strong>Commutation Recovery Impact:</strong> Because monthly Dearness Relief (DR) is always calculated on the full unreduced revised basic pension, the higher multiplier delivers enhanced monthly liquidity even for seniors undergoing active commutation deductions.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Adoption by State Governments (Tamil Nadu, UP, Maharashtra & Beyond)</h2>
        <p>
          While Central Pay Commissions formally govern central civil servants, railways, postal staff, and defence formations, state administrations across <strong>Tamil Nadu</strong>, <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong>, <strong>Maharashtra</strong>, <strong>Rajasthan</strong>, <strong>Bihar</strong>, and <strong>Karnataka</strong> reference the finalized central fitment factor through their respective State Pay Revision Committees.
        </p>
        <p>
